# Task class: customer-support ticket resolution drafting.
#
# Not the shipped mixed.jsonl. This is my own domain for the S15 budget-policy
# assignment: given one raw support ticket (a short, sometimes messy customer
# message), draft the reply a support agent would send — including the correct
# action classification, in a form that is ready to send with no further
# editing. This is exactly the shape of task real support teams route through
# a capability ladder: trivial FAQ-lookup tickets should never touch a frontier
# model, but multi-issue, policy-adjacent, or ambiguous tickets need one.
#
# One JSON object per line: {"id", "task", "expectation", "difficulty"}.
# "expectation" is a short natural-language success criterion the generic
# judge rubric (config/evals.yaml) scores against — never an answer key the
# code matches on. "difficulty" is a free-form label used only to group the
# report; nothing branches on it.
#
# 18 tasks: 8 trivial, 6 moderate, 4 hard — deliberately spanning difficulty so
# cost-per-resolved-task can diverge from cost-per-call. Every ticket is
# self-contained, needs no tool or account lookup, and has a checkable result.

{"id": "s01_password_reset", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"Hi, I forgot my password and the reset email never arrived. Can you help?\" Write the reply an agent would send.", "expectation": "Tells the customer to check spam/junk, gives the concrete step of using the 'forgot password' link, and offers to manually trigger a new reset email if it still does not arrive. Does not ask the customer for their password."}
{"id": "s02_refund_status", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"I requested a refund 5 days ago (order #48213) and haven't heard anything.\" Refunds are stated to take 3-5 business days to appear once approved. Write the reply.", "expectation": "Acknowledges the order number, states the 3-5 business day refund window, and tells the customer what to do if it does not appear by day 5 (contact support again / escalate), without inventing a specific new date."}
{"id": "s03_cancel_subscription", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"How do I cancel my monthly subscription?\" Write the reply.", "expectation": "Gives a concrete, actionable path to cancel (e.g. account settings > subscription > cancel) and states that access continues until the end of the current billing period."}
{"id": "s04_invoice_copy", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"Can you email me a copy of last month's invoice for my accounting team?\" Write the reply.", "expectation": "Confirms the invoice will be sent or explains exactly where the customer can download it themselves (billing/invoices section), and does not ask for information the company should already have (like the account email)."}
{"id": "s05_shipping_delay", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"My order was supposed to arrive yesterday and tracking hasn't updated in 3 days.\" Write the reply.", "expectation": "Apologises for the delay, states a concrete next step (checking with the carrier / offering a replacement or refund if it does not arrive by a stated point), and does not blame the customer."}
{"id": "s06_update_email", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"I need to change the email address on my account from an old work email to my personal one.\" Write the reply.", "expectation": "Gives the concrete self-serve path (account settings > email) and notes that a verification email will need to be confirmed at the new address, OR explains what identity verification is needed if support must do it manually."}
{"id": "s07_feature_request", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"Any chance you could add dark mode to the mobile app? My eyes are dying at night.\" Write the reply.", "expectation": "Thanks the customer for the suggestion, states clearly whether this is already available, planned, or will be logged as feedback, and does not promise a specific ship date that was not given."}
{"id": "s08_where_is_download", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"I paid for the ebook but can't find the download link anywhere.\" Write the reply.", "expectation": "Gives the concrete location of the download (order confirmation email / account > purchases / library) and offers a fallback (resend the link) if the customer still cannot find it."}
{"id": "s09_double_charge", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"I was charged twice for my order #77210 on the same day, once for $49.99 and once for $49.99. My bank statement shows both. I need this fixed.\" Write the reply, including what happens next and roughly when.", "expectation": "Acknowledges the duplicate charge as an error to be corrected (not disputed), states that one of the two charges will be refunded, gives a concrete timeframe for the refund to appear, and does NOT ask the customer to dispute it with their bank first."}
{"id": "s10_partial_delivery", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"Order #55102 was supposed to have 3 items (a lamp, a rug, and a mirror). Only the lamp and rug arrived. The mirror is missing from the box and the packing slip.\" Write the reply.", "expectation": "Identifies that this is a fulfillment/shortage issue rather than a lost-package issue, commits to a concrete resolution (ship the missing mirror or refund for it), and asks nothing of the customer that is unreasonable (e.g. does not ask them to prove the box was empty with photos if not necessary)."}
{"id": "s11_downgrade_and_credit", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"I want to downgrade from the Pro plan ($40/mo) to the Basic plan ($15/mo) starting next cycle, and I'd like to know if I get credited for the unused days on my current cycle.\" State a policy: downgrades take effect at the next renewal and no partial-period credit is issued for downgrades (only for cancellations within 14 days of signup). Write the reply.", "expectation": "Correctly states BOTH parts: the downgrade takes effect at next renewal (not immediately), and no partial credit is issued for downgrades under the stated policy — while distinguishing that from the unrelated 14-day cancellation credit so the customer is not misled into thinking they qualify."}
{"id": "s12_two_issues_one_ticket", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"Two things: first, my coupon code SAVE20 isn't working at checkout. Second, once that's sorted, can you confirm if the item ships internationally to Canada?\" Write a reply addressing both.", "expectation": "Addresses both the coupon issue (offers a concrete next step: check expiry/eligibility or apply it manually) AND the shipping-to-Canada question (a clear yes/no or how to find out) as two distinct, clearly separated answers — neither question is dropped."}
{"id": "s13_angry_escalation", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"This is the THIRD time I'm writing about my broken order #91820. Nobody has fixed anything. I want a manager and a full refund NOW.\" The policy is that a full refund is warranted here since 2 prior tickets on the same order are unresolved. Write the reply.", "expectation": "Acknowledges the repeated contact and the frustration without being defensive, grants the full refund clearly and concretely (not conditionally), and addresses the request to escalate to a manager honestly rather than ignoring it."}
{"id": "s14_ambiguous_billing_cycle", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"I signed up on the 15th but got charged again on the 1st of next month, only 16 days later, not a full month. Is that right?\" Billing policy: paid plans renew on the 1st of each calendar month regardless of signup date, and the first charge is prorated for the partial period; the charge on the 1st is the first FULL month, which is correct and expected. Write the reply.", "expectation": "Correctly explains that billing aligns to the 1st of the calendar month (not to the signup anniversary), that the short first period was a prorated partial month, and that the charge on the 1st is expected and correct — not an error — without contradicting itself."}
{"id": "s15_data_export_deadline", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"My account was suspended for a Terms of Service violation and I have 30 days to export my data before deletion, per your policy. I was suspended on 2026-07-20. Today is 2026-08-14. How many days do I have left, and can you also confirm exactly what data formats are available for export (the help center says CSV and JSON, but a colleague was told only CSV)?\" Write the reply, doing the date arithmetic and resolving the formats discrepancy by trusting the documented policy (CSV and JSON) over hearsay.", "expectation": "Correctly computes the days remaining (30 days from 2026-07-20 is 2026-08-19, so 5 days remain from 2026-08-14, inclusive framing acceptable if stated clearly), and resolves the formats question by confirming CSV AND JSON are both available (per documented policy), addressing the discrepancy directly rather than ignoring it."}
{"id": "s16_conflicting_policies", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"Your website says free returns within 30 days, but the packing slip I received says 14 days. I'm on day 20. Which is it, and can I return this?\" State a resolution rule: when policies conflict, the customer-facing published policy at time of purchase (the website, 30 days) governs over an internal document (the packing slip), so this return should be accepted. Write the reply.", "expectation": "Explicitly acknowledges the conflict between the two stated policies, resolves it in the customer's favor per the given rule (website policy of 30 days governs), states clearly that the return on day 20 is accepted, and does not leave the customer with an ambiguous 'it depends' answer."}
{"id": "s17_multi_order_refund_math", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"I have two orders from the same week: order A for $120.00 that I want fully refunded, and order B for $75.50 where I only want to return one of two items, the $32.25 one. What's my total refund across both, and will it go back to the same card?\" Assume refunds always return to the original payment method. Write the reply with the correct total.", "expectation": "Computes the correct total refund of $152.25 (120.00 + 32.25), states it as one clear number tied to both orders, and confirms the refund goes back to the original payment method — the arithmetic must be exactly right, not approximate."}
{"id": "s18_tiered_discount_eligibility", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Draft a support reply to this ticket: \"I've spent $340 total on your store this year across 4 orders. Your loyalty program gives 5% off for $250+/year and 10% off for $500+/year, applied automatically at checkout on the NEXT qualifying order once a tier is reached. Am I getting a discount on my next order, and if so how much?\" Write the reply with the correct tier and percentage.", "expectation": "Correctly identifies that $340 qualifies for the 5% tier (over $250 but under $500), states the discount will be 5% (not 10%) and will apply automatically on the next order, and does not overstate eligibility for the 10% tier."}
